const SCI_UNITS = {
  s71: {
    title: "Lesson 7.1",
    subtitle: "Fossils",
    emoji: "🦴",
    cssClass: "sci",
    bigQuestion: "What is a fossil, and how do fossils form?",
    vocab: [
      { w: "fossil", def: "the remains or trace of a living thing that lived very long ago" },
      { w: "extinct", def: "no longer living anywhere on Earth" },
      { w: "evidence", def: "information that supports that something is true" },
      { w: "trace fossil", def: "a fossil that shows a sign of an animal — like a footprint, burrow, nest, eggshell, or dung — instead of its body" },
      { w: "amber", def: "honey-colored hardened tree resin that can preserve insects or small plants as fossils" },
      { w: "mold", def: "the empty print left in rock when a dead organism's body decays" },
      { w: "cast", def: "a fossil formed when minerals fill in a mold and harden" },
      { w: "sediment", def: "mud, sand, or tiny pieces of rock that can bury an organism and help make a fossil" },
      { w: "paleontologist", def: "a scientist who digs up and studies fossils to learn about ancient life" }
    ],
    lesson: [
      "<h4>The Big Question</h4><p>A <strong>fossil</strong> is the <strong>remains or trace</strong> of a living thing that lived very long ago. Some fossils are millions of years old. Scientists study fossils to learn about plants and animals from long ago.</p>",
      "<h4>What Counts as a Fossil?</h4>" +
      "<p>Most fossils form from the <strong>hard parts</strong> of living things — bones, shells, and teeth. Soft parts usually decay too quickly to fossilize. A <strong>trace fossil</strong> is a footprint, burrow, eggshell, nest, or dung. Trace fossils give clues about what animals <em>did</em> — how they walked, what they ate, or where they lived.</p>" +
      "<p>Most organisms do <strong>not</strong> become fossils. They decay or are eaten after they die. Fossils form only in special conditions — usually when the body or trace is <strong>quickly buried</strong> by mud, sand, or other material at the bottom of rivers, swamps, lakes, or oceans.</p>",
      "<h4>How Does a Fossil Form? (5 Steps)</h4>" +
      "<p><strong>1.</strong> A fish dies in the ocean. Its body sinks to the ocean floor.<br>" +
      "<strong>2.</strong> The body is quickly covered with layers of mud or sand.<br>" +
      "<strong>3.</strong> Water flows through the rock and dissolves the remains. The empty space left behind is a <strong>fossil mold</strong>.<br>" +
      "<strong>4.</strong> Minerals enter the mold and harden over many years. This forms a <strong>cast fossil</strong>. Layers of mud build up and form rock.<br>" +
      "<strong>5.</strong> After millions of years, the ocean changes and the land changes too. The fossil ends up in rock that may be far from the ocean.</p>",
      "<h4>Fossils in Sap, Ice, and Tar</h4>" +
      "<p>Not all fossils are in rock. Tree resin can ooze down a tree, trap insects, and harden into <strong>amber</strong> — these fossils can be over 100 million years old.</p>" +
      "<p><strong>Frozen fossils</strong> form when animals get trapped in ice. Woolly mammoths frozen in ice still have soft parts preserved, so scientists can learn how they moved and what they ate.</p>" +
      "<p><strong>Tar pits</strong> are areas filled with sticky tar that seeps up from inside Earth (like the La Brea tar pits in California). Animals got trapped in the tar, and the tar preserved their skeletons.</p>",
      "<h4>Extinct Organisms</h4>" +
      "<p>Many of the kinds of organisms that formed fossils are <strong>extinct</strong> — they no longer live on Earth. <strong>Dinosaurs</strong> are extinct. Today scientists find their fossilized bones and learn a lot about them.</p>",
      "<h4>The Story in the Ground (4 Steps)</h4>" +
      "<p>The Fossil Explorer's Guide shows us a simpler way to remember how fossils form:</p>" +
      "<p><strong>1. Die &amp; Sink</strong> — An animal dies in water and sinks to the bottom.<br>" +
      "<strong>2. Buried Fast</strong> — <strong>Sediment</strong> (mud and sand) covers it quickly.<br>" +
      "<strong>3. Time &amp; Pressure</strong> — Over millions of years, the mud turns into hard rock. The remains become a fossil.<br>" +
      "<strong>4. Discovery!</strong> — A scientist called a <strong>paleontologist</strong> digs it up and studies it.</p>",
      "<h4>The Sticky Tree Story (How Amber Forms)</h4>" +
      "<p>Amber fossils form in three steps: <strong>1. Tree Sap</strong> — a plant makes sticky juice to heal a cut on its bark. <strong>2. Bug Stuck</strong> — a tiny insect lands on the sap and gets trapped. <strong>3. Hardens</strong> — the sap dries, gets buried, and turns into an amber fossil over millions of years.</p>",
      "<h4>Why Are Fossils So Hard to Find?</h4>" +
      "<p>Most animals do <strong>not</strong> become fossils — it takes perfect conditions! After an animal dies, three things usually happen instead:</p>" +
      "<p><strong>Path 1:</strong> The body gets <strong>eaten or rots away</strong>.<br>" +
      "<strong>Path 2:</strong> It gets <strong>broken by rain and wind</strong>.<br>" +
      "<strong>Path 3:</strong> It is <strong>not buried fast enough</strong> before it falls apart.</p>" +
      "<p>Fossils are rare puzzles — only creatures with perfect luck get preserved!</p>",
      "<h4>Amazing Fossil Finds!</h4>" +
      "<p>Scientists are finding new fossils all the time. Some incredible examples:</p>" +
      "<p>🦖 <strong>T-Rex</strong> — about <strong>65 million years old</strong><br>" +
      "🌿 <strong>Fern Plant</strong> — about <strong>400 million years old</strong><br>" +
      "💀 <strong>Early Human</strong> — about <strong>10,000 years old</strong></p>" +
      "<p>What fossil will scientists find next?</p>"
    ],
    quiz_mc: [
      { q: "What is a <strong>fossil</strong>?", options: ["a kind of dinosaur", "the remains or trace of a living thing that lived very long ago", "a rock with shiny minerals", "a kind of tree resin"], answer: 1, explain: "A fossil is the remains or trace of a living thing that lived very long ago." },
      { q: "Which of these is a <strong>trace fossil</strong>?", options: ["a dinosaur tooth", "a fish skeleton", "a footprint in mud that turned to rock", "a piece of amber"], answer: 2, explain: "Trace fossils are signs an animal made — footprints, burrows, eggshells, nests, or dung." },
      { q: "Which is most likely to become a fossil?", options: ["the soft body of a worm on dry land", "a fish quickly buried in mud at the bottom of a lake", "a leaf that blows around in the wind", "an animal eaten by other animals"], answer: 1, explain: "Fossils form best when an organism is quickly buried — like at the bottom of a lake or ocean." },
      { q: "<strong>Amber</strong> fossils form when…", options: ["minerals fill an empty mold", "tree resin traps insects and hardens", "ice freezes a mammoth's body", "tar pits trap large animals"], answer: 1, explain: "Amber is hardened tree resin. It can preserve insects or small plants for over 100 million years." },
      { q: "When scientists find <strong>woolly mammoths frozen in ice</strong>, what is special about these fossils?", options: ["They are made entirely of minerals.", "Their soft body parts are still preserved.", "They are millions of years old.", "They formed in tar pits."], answer: 1, explain: "Frozen mammoths still have soft parts — so scientists can learn how they moved, what they ate, and what shape their bodies were." },
      { q: "Which of these animals is <strong>extinct</strong>?", options: ["lion", "dinosaur", "elephant", "horse"], answer: 1, explain: "Dinosaurs no longer live on Earth — they are extinct." },
      { q: "Most fossils form from the <strong>___</strong> of living things.", options: ["soft skin and muscles", "blood and feathers", "hard parts like bones, shells, and teeth", "leaves and flowers"], answer: 2, explain: "Most fossils come from hard parts — bones, shells, and teeth — because soft parts decay too quickly." },
      { q: "A <strong>fossil mold</strong> is…", options: ["a fuzzy plant that grows on rocks", "the empty print left when a body decays", "a kind of tar pit", "a mammoth frozen in ice"], answer: 1, explain: "When the body decays inside rock, it leaves an empty print — a mold. If minerals fill it, you get a cast." },
      { q: "Where do fossils <strong>most often</strong> form?", options: ["on mountain tops", "on the bottom of rivers, swamps, lakes, or oceans", "in dry deserts", "in volcanoes"], answer: 1, explain: "Fossils form most often on the bottom of rivers, swamps, lakes, or oceans — where bodies get buried quickly by mud or sand." },
      { q: "The La Brea <strong>tar pits</strong> in California are famous because…", options: ["they preserve skeletons of animals trapped in tar", "they have the oldest amber fossils", "they are the only place mammoths are found", "they are made of frozen ice"], answer: 0, explain: "Tar from inside Earth seeps up at La Brea. Animals got stuck and the tar preserved their skeletons." },
      { q: "What is <strong>sediment</strong>?", options: ["a type of dinosaur", "mud, sand, or tiny pieces of rock that can bury an organism", "hardened tree sap", "a scientist who studies fossils"], answer: 1, explain: "Sediment — mud and sand — covers a dead animal quickly and helps start the fossilization process." },
      { q: "What is a <strong>paleontologist</strong>?", options: ["a scientist who studies weather", "a scientist who makes fossils", "a scientist who digs up and studies fossils", "a scientist who studies living animals"], answer: 2, explain: "Paleontologists dig up fossils and study them to learn about ancient life on Earth." },
      { q: "In the 4-step fossil story, what happens in Step 2?", options: ["A paleontologist digs up the fossil", "The animal dies and sinks", "Sediment covers the body quickly", "Mud turns into hard rock"], answer: 2, explain: "Step 2 is 'Buried Fast' — sediment (mud and sand) covers the body quickly so it can be preserved." },
      { q: "In the <strong>amber story</strong>, why does the tree make sticky sap in the first place?", options: ["To trap insects on purpose", "To make honey for animals to eat", "To heal a cut on its bark", "To attract paleontologists"], answer: 2, explain: "The tree makes sticky sap to heal a cut on its bark — the insect getting trapped is an accident!" },
      { q: "Which of these is NOT a reason most animals do NOT become fossils?", options: ["They get eaten or rot away", "They get broken by rain and wind", "They are not buried fast enough", "They are too heavy to sink in water"], answer: 3, explain: "The three paths that prevent fossilization are: eaten/rotted, broken by weather, or not buried fast enough. Weight is not the issue." },
      { q: "About how old is a T-Rex fossil?", options: ["10,000 years old", "400 million years old", "65 million years old", "1 million years old"], answer: 2, explain: "T-Rex fossils are about 65 million years old — from the end of the Mesozoic Era." },
      { q: "Which fossil find is the <strong>oldest</strong>?", options: ["Early human at 10,000 years old", "T-Rex at 65 million years old", "Fern plant at 400 million years old", "Woolly mammoth frozen in ice"], answer: 2, explain: "The fern plant fossil at 400 million years old is the oldest — even older than dinosaurs!" }
    ],
    quiz_tf: [
      { q: "Most living things become fossils after they die.", answer: false, explain: "False — most organisms decay or are eaten. Fossils form only in special conditions." },
      { q: "A footprint that turned to rock is called a <strong>trace fossil</strong>.", answer: true, explain: "True — footprints, burrows, eggshells, and dung are all trace fossils." },
      { q: "Soft body parts fossilize <strong>more easily</strong> than hard parts.", answer: false, explain: "False — hard parts like bones and shells fossilize most easily. Soft parts usually decay first." },
      { q: "<strong>Amber</strong> is hardened tree resin that can trap insects.", answer: true, explain: "True — and amber fossils can be more than 100 million years old." },
      { q: "<strong>Extinct</strong> means an animal is endangered but still alive.", answer: false, explain: "False — extinct means the animal no longer lives on Earth at all." },
      { q: "Dinosaurs are <strong>extinct</strong>.", answer: true, explain: "True — dinosaurs no longer live on Earth, but their fossilized bones remain." },
      { q: "When minerals fill in a fossil mold and harden, the result is called a <strong>cast</strong>.", answer: true, explain: "True — minerals enter the mold and form a cast fossil over many years." },
      { q: "Frozen mammoths are useful to scientists because their <strong>soft parts</strong> are preserved.", answer: true, explain: "True — frozen soft parts tell scientists how the animal moved, what it ate, and its body shape." },
      { q: "Tar pits preserve fossils by <strong>freezing</strong> the animals.", answer: false, explain: "False — tar pits trap animals in sticky tar, not ice." },
      { q: "Fossils most often form at the bottom of <strong>rivers, swamps, lakes, or oceans</strong>.", answer: true, explain: "True — bodies are buried quickly there before they can decay." },
      { q: "<strong>Sediment</strong> is mud or sand that can bury an organism and help make a fossil.", answer: true, explain: "True — sediment covers the body in Step 2 of fossil formation." },
      { q: "A <strong>paleontologist</strong> is a scientist who digs up and studies fossils.", answer: true, explain: "True — paleontologists are the fossil experts who discover and study ancient remains." },
      { q: "Most animals that die will eventually become fossils.", answer: false, explain: "False — most bodies get eaten, rot away, or are broken by weather. Only a very lucky few become fossils." },
      { q: "In the amber story, the tree makes sap on purpose to trap insects.", answer: false, explain: "False — the tree makes sap to heal a cut on its bark. Trapping the insect is an accident!" },
      { q: "A T-Rex fossil is older than a fern plant fossil.", answer: false, explain: "False — T-Rex fossils are about 65 million years old, but fern plant fossils can be 400 million years old." },
      { q: "An early human fossil is about 10,000 years old.", answer: true, explain: "True — early human fossils are much younger than dinosaur or plant fossils." }
    ],
    quiz_fill: [
      { q: "A ___ is the remains or trace of a living thing that lived very long ago.", options: ["mineral", "fossil", "rock", "shell"], answer: 1, explain: "Fossil — remains or trace of a long-ago living thing." },
      { q: "An animal that no longer lives on Earth is ___.", options: ["endangered", "ancient", "extinct", "frozen"], answer: 2, explain: "Extinct = no longer living anywhere on Earth." },
      { q: "A footprint or burrow that became a fossil is called a ___ fossil.", options: ["bone", "trace", "amber", "cast"], answer: 1, explain: "Trace fossil — a sign an animal made, not its body." },
      { q: "Honey-colored hardened tree resin that traps insects is called ___.", options: ["amber", "tar", "ice", "mud"], answer: 0, explain: "Amber preserves small living things over 100 million years." },
      { q: "Information that supports that something is true is called ___.", options: ["a fossil", "evidence", "an opinion", "a guess"], answer: 1, explain: "Evidence supports a claim — like fossil footprints showing an animal walked there." },
      { q: "When minerals fill a mold and harden, they form a ___.", options: ["cast", "shell", "tar pit", "burrow"], answer: 0, explain: "Cast fossil — the mold gets filled and hardens." },
      { q: "The ___ pits in California preserved skeletons in sticky tar.", options: ["Mammoth", "La Brea", "Amber", "Mojave"], answer: 1, explain: "La Brea tar pits in California." },
      { q: "Most fossils form from the ___ parts of living things.", options: ["soft", "hard", "wet", "warm"], answer: 1, explain: "Hard parts — bones, shells, teeth — fossilize most easily." },
      { q: "Mammoths frozen in ice still have ___ parts preserved.", options: ["soft", "rocky", "extinct", "amber"], answer: 0, explain: "Frozen soft parts tell scientists about diet, movement, and body shape." },
      { q: "Mud, sand, or tiny pieces of rock that help bury organisms are called ___.", options: ["amber", "sediment", "tar", "cast"], answer: 1, explain: "Sediment is the mud and sand that covers a body quickly — key to fossil formation." },
      { q: "A scientist who digs up and studies fossils is called a ___.", options: ["biologist", "geologist", "paleontologist", "zoologist"], answer: 2, explain: "Paleontologist — the fossil expert!" },
      { q: "In the 4-step fossil story, Step 3 is 'Time and ___.'", options: ["Pressure", "Water", "Wind", "Sand"], answer: 0, explain: "Time and Pressure — mud gets squashed and hardens into rock over millions of years." },
      { q: "A T-Rex fossil is about ___ million years old.", options: ["10", "400", "65", "1"], answer: 2, explain: "T-Rex lived about 65 million years ago, at the end of the Mesozoic Era." },
      { q: "Fossils are rare because most animals get ___ or rot away.", options: ["frozen", "eaten", "buried", "trapped"], answer: 1, explain: "Being eaten or rotting is Path 1 — the most common reason animals don't become fossils." }
    ],
    challenge_questions: [
      { q: "Scientists find SEASHELL fossils in rock at the top of a mountain. What's the MOST LIKELY explanation?", options: ["Hikers carrying shells dropped them on the mountain over many years.", "The mountain area was once underwater before being lifted up.", "Birds carried the shells up to the peak from beaches far below.", "The seashells slowly grew there over time without any ocean involved."], answer: 1, explain: "Marine fossils embedded in rock are evidence that the area was once sea floor. Tectonic forces lifted those rocks over millions of years." },
      { q: "Why do BONES and SHELLS fossilize more often than skin or muscle?", options: ["Hard parts resist decay long enough to be replaced by minerals.", "Bones and shells are larger than soft tissues in most organisms.", "Hard parts have brighter, more visible colors when buried in rock.", "Bones and shells are heavier and tend to sink down into sediment."], answer: 0, explain: "Soft tissue rots within weeks. Hard parts last long enough for the slow process of mineralization to preserve them as stone." },
      { q: "Fossils of palm trees are found in ANTARCTICA today. What does this suggest?", options: ["Some palm species can survive in extremely cold and icy conditions.", "Someone planted those palm-tree fossils in Antarctica fairly recently.", "Antarctica was once located in a much warmer climate than it is now.", "Old palm-tree fossils can move slowly on their own across continents."], answer: 2, explain: "Antarctica was once near the equator with a warm climate. Fossils preserve evidence of past climates that look nothing like today's." },
      { q: "What's the KEY DIFFERENCE between a BODY fossil and a TRACE fossil?", options: ["Body fossils are noticeably bigger than trace fossils most of the time.", "There is no real difference between the two — the words are interchangeable.", "Trace fossils are in every case older than the body fossils nearby.", "Body fossils preserve remains; trace fossils preserve activity (tracks, burrows)."], answer: 3, explain: "Body fossils tell us what an organism LOOKED like; trace fossils tell us what it DID. Both are valuable, complementary evidence." },
      { q: "Why are AMBER FOSSILS unusually well-preserved?", options: ["Amber is a harder material than most types of fossil-bearing rock.", "Tree sap sealed organisms quickly before any decay could begin.", "Amber's bright orange color helps protect insects trapped inside it.", "Amber is rare, so the few amber fossils get extra-careful preservation work."], answer: 1, explain: "Sticky resin trapped insects and small organisms, then hardened — sealing them from oxygen, water, and bacteria for millions of years." },
      { q: "Some species today look almost the same as their 60-million-year-old fossils. What does that mean?", options: ["Those fossils are fakes that scientists have failed to identify yet.", "Time has stopped for those particular species, freezing them in place.", "Some species change very little when their environment stays stable.", "Every species on Earth is hundreds of millions of years older than thought."], answer: 2, explain: "When environments stay stable, evolutionary pressure for change is low. These 'living fossils' (like horseshoe crabs) survived because their form already worked." },
      { q: "How did the LA BREA TAR PITS preserve so many Ice Age fossils?", options: ["Sticky tar trapped animals and then sealed them away from oxygen.", "The tar pits were extremely cold, freezing animals that fell into them.", "Early scientists deliberately buried Ice Age animal remains in the tar.", "The tar in the pits was magnetic and pulled in nearby animal bones."], answer: 0, explain: "Animals stepped into the tar, couldn't escape, and were preserved when the tar sealed them from decay-causing oxygen and microbes." },
      { q: "What's the BIGGEST CHALLENGE when reconstructing a dinosaur from fossils?", options: ["Most dinosaur bones are too heavy for paleontologists to safely move.", "Dinosaur bones are usually too clean and lack any kind of useful detail.", "There are far too many complete dinosaur fossils to study them all.", "Most fossils are incomplete fragments rather than full skeletons."], answer: 3, explain: "Paleontologists rarely find a complete skeleton. They reconstruct from partial bones plus comparison to known relatives — informed guesswork." },
      { q: "Why do DEEPER ROCK LAYERS usually contain OLDER fossils?", options: ["Sediment piles up over time, leaving newer layers on top of older ones.", "Older fossils are heavier than newer ones and sink down through rock.", "Younger fossils slowly evaporate and disappear from the upper layers.", "Geologic layers are always being reshuffled by underground forces."], answer: 0, explain: "This is the law of superposition — sediments build up over time, so deeper = older (unless rocks have been disturbed by tectonic activity)." },
      { q: "If a place has both DINOSAUR and MAMMOTH fossils, what does it suggest?", options: ["Dinosaurs and mammoths were friends and shared the same habitat.", "Both species could travel through time, meeting in the same locations.", "One of the two species hunted and ate the other one for food.", "They lived in the same area but separated by millions of years."], answer: 3, explain: "Dinosaurs went extinct ~65 million years ago. Mammoths lived ~10,000 years ago. Same location, vastly different times — the layers tell the story." },
      { q: "Why are fossils sometimes called a 'WINDOW INTO THE PAST'?", options: ["Many of them are found in window-shaped rectangles inside rock.", "They show what life and environments used to be like long ago.", "Fossils literally open and close like little windows when handled.", "Scientists found them often by looking through real glass windows."], answer: 1, explain: "Fossils give us direct evidence of life forms and environments that no longer exist — the closest thing we have to seeing the deep past." },
      { q: "What's WRONG with the idea that 'all fossils come from DINOSAURS'?", options: ["Dinosaurs are still alive in remote areas of the Earth even today.", "Dinosaurs were never real — they were invented by paleontologists.", "Most fossils are plants, microbes, or invertebrates, not dinosaurs.", "There aren't actually any fossils on Earth — fossils don't really exist."], answer: 2, explain: "Dinosaurs are famous, but they're a tiny fraction of the fossil record. Bacteria, ferns, trilobites, and shellfish far outnumber them." },
      { q: "Why do FROZEN MAMMOTHS give scientists more information than typical rock fossils?", options: ["Frozen mammoths are easier to date with modern scientific instruments.", "Frozen mammoths are bigger than rock fossils and so easier to study.", "Soft tissue, hair, and stomach contents stay preserved in frozen specimens.", "Frozen mammoths smell better than rock fossils when scientists handle them."], answer: 2, explain: "Permafrost preserves DNA, last meals, fur color, and even disease evidence — details that rock fossils almost never preserve." },
      { q: "Why do paleontologists CAREFULLY RECORD the rock layer where a fossil is found?", options: ["Recording layers makes paleontology look more scientific to the public.", "Every state in the U.S. legally requires recording of every fossil layer.", "Geologic layers are pretty and worth photographing for art purposes.", "The layer where a fossil sits helps determine the fossil's actual age."], answer: 3, explain: "Stratigraphy uses rock-layer position to estimate age. Removing a fossil without recording its layer destroys crucial dating information." },
      { q: "What can a FOSSIL TOOTH'S SHAPE tell scientists?", options: ["Whether the animal ate mostly plants, mostly meat, or a mix of both.", "The exact age that the individual animal was at the moment it died.", "What color the animal's fur, feathers, or skin was when it was alive.", "The specific name that scientists eventually give to the animal species."], answer: 0, explain: "Sharp pointed teeth = carnivore. Flat grinding teeth = herbivore. Mixed teeth = omnivore. Tooth structure follows diet." },
      { q: "Why do scientists prefer MULTIPLE lines of evidence over just ONE?", options: ["Using more lines of evidence makes scientific publications look more impressive.", "Multiple sources cross-check each other and reduce the chance of errors.", "Using only one source has been a violation of scientific tradition for centuries.", "Each new line of evidence costs more money, helping the scientific economy."], answer: 1, explain: "If fossils, rock layers, and modern analogs all point the same direction, the conclusion is much stronger than any one source alone." },
      { q: "What does it mean for a species to be EXTINCT?", options: ["The species is hiding from observers but still exists somewhere unseen.", "All members of the species are gone forever and cannot return.", "Members of the species are sleeping deeply but will eventually wake.", "The species has moved away to another continent without telling anyone."], answer: 1, explain: "Extinction is permanent. Once the last individual dies, the species cannot return through normal biological processes." },
      { q: "Which is a NATURAL cause of extinction?", options: ["A species deciding to ignore its main food source for a long time.", "An animal completely forgetting how to walk on its legs anymore.", "Sudden climate change that destroys a species' habitat very quickly.", "A species choosing to be too friendly to its natural predators."], answer: 2, explain: "Climate shifts, asteroid impacts, volcanic eruptions, new predators — all natural pressures that can wipe out species that can't adapt fast enough." },
      { q: "What's the difference between a FOSSIL and an ARTIFACT?", options: ["Fossils are always larger in size than human-made artifacts can be.", "Artifacts are always older than fossils that come from the same area.", "Fossils come from once-living things; artifacts are objects humans made.", "There is really no important difference between fossils and artifacts."], answer: 2, explain: "Paleontology studies fossils (life). Archaeology studies artifacts (human-made objects). Different fields with different methods." },
      { q: "Why is a fossil's LOCATION almost as important as the fossil itself?", options: ["Geographic locations make the most beautiful illustrations in textbooks.", "Most paleontologists collect maps of fossil sites as a personal hobby.", "Recording locations is required for transporting fossils across borders.", "The location reveals when and where the organism actually lived."], answer: 3, explain: "A fossil out of context loses much of its scientific value. Where + when + with-what is the full story; the bone alone is incomplete." },
      { q: "Why do MOST organisms never become fossils?", options: ["Most organisms are too small to leave any trace in the fossil record.", "Decay almost always destroys their bodies before fossilization can happen.", "Most organisms simply prefer not to leave fossil remains behind them.", "Fossils are rare because nature has decided to keep them rare on purpose."], answer: 1, explain: "Fossilization requires unusual conditions — rapid burial, low oxygen, mineral-rich water. Most dead organisms are eaten or rot completely." },
      { q: "What can a fossil's SIZE help scientists guess about an ancient animal?", options: ["The exact name that scientists will assign to the species.", "The specific day on which that individual animal died.", "The animal's diet, likely habitat, and overall body type.", "The animal's favorite color while it was alive on Earth."], answer: 2, explain: "Body size correlates with food needs, range, predators — clues paleontologists use to reconstruct ancient lifestyles." },
      { q: "Why is finding a TRANSITIONAL fossil exciting for biologists?", options: ["Transitional fossils show features between two known groups — evidence of evolution.", "Transitional fossils tend to be much prettier and more colorful than other fossils.", "Transitional fossils almost always sell for very high prices to collectors.", "Transitional fossils are noticeably easier to find than other fossil types."], answer: 0, explain: "Transitional fossils (like Tiktaalik, a fish-amphibian) show direct evidence that one group of organisms gave rise to another over time." },
      { q: "What can fossils NOT directly tell us about extinct animals?", options: ["The general shape of the bones in the animal's skeleton.", "The exact sound an animal made when it was alive long ago.", "The size of an animal's teeth and the shape of those teeth.", "The approximate body length the animal had at adult size."], answer: 1, explain: "Soft tissues, behaviors, and sounds usually don't fossilize. Scientists can guess from anatomy, but direct evidence of voices is rare." },
      { q: "How can MARINE FOSSIL distribution help map ancient coastlines?", options: ["Marine fossils glow brightly in the dark, marking old shorelines clearly.", "Areas with marine fossils were probably once underwater or near the sea.", "Marine fossils slowly float to the surface, marking ancient shorelines today.", "Marine fossils show where ancient boats sailed thousands of years ago."], answer: 1, explain: "If marine fossils are found in a band across a continent, that band was likely once a coastline or shallow sea. Their distribution maps lost geography." },
      { q: "Why do scientists compare MODERN animals to FOSSIL ancestors?", options: ["Living relatives can hint at how the extinct ones likely behaved.", "Comparing modern animals to fossils saves a great deal of research time.", "Federal law requires every fossil study to include a modern comparison.", "Modern animals are physically identical to fossils in every detail."], answer: 0, explain: "Uniformitarianism: physical and biological processes today are similar to those in the past. Studying living relatives gives clues about extinct cousins." },
      { q: "If MANY of ONE species are found dead in one small spot, what could it mean?", options: ["The animals were holding a large meeting at that location together.", "A herd was caught by a sudden event such as a flood or tar pit.", "The animals were all eating dinner in the same spot when they died.", "The animals were peacefully sleeping together at that location at death."], answer: 1, explain: "Mass-death assemblages often indicate a sudden event — a flash flood, volcanic ash, or sticky trap caught a group at once." },
      { q: "Why are some fossils VERY detailed while others are blurry?", options: ["The most detailed fossils are usually much newer than the blurry ones.", "Special conditions (fine sediment, low oxygen) preserve fine details well.", "The most detailed fossils were carefully polished by humans long ago.", "The level of detail in fossils depends on the phase of the moon at burial."], answer: 1, explain: "Lagerstätten — fossil sites with extraordinary preservation — form under unusual conditions. Most fossils form less perfectly." },
      { q: "What is a 'LIVING FOSSIL'?", options: ["A fossil that visibly wiggles when scientists touch it in the laboratory.", "A pretend fossil that appeared in a famous popular movie or TV show.", "A modern species nearly identical to its much older fossil ancestors.", "Any fossil that has been newly discovered within the past few weeks."], answer: 2, explain: "Horseshoe crabs and coelacanths are 'living fossils' — modern species whose form has changed remarkably little over hundreds of millions of years." },
      { q: "Why does the FOSSIL RECORD support the idea that Earth is VERY OLD?", options: ["Old fossils glow brightly, showing their age to scientists in dark labs.", "The fossil record shows millions of years of slowly changing life forms.", "Old fossils whisper their actual age to paleontologists who hold them.", "Fossils are radioactive and emit a clear age signal that can be measured."], answer: 1, explain: "Layer upon layer of distinctly different fossils, with measurable ages spanning billions of years, requires Earth to be MUCH older than a few thousand years." }
    ]
  },

  s72: {
    title: "Lesson 7.2",
    subtitle: "Fossils as a Record",
    emoji: "📜",
    cssClass: "sci",
    bigQuestion: "How do scientists use fossils to learn about Earth's past?",
    vocab: [
      { w: "fossil record", def: "the total of all fossils that have been found, organized in units of time" },
      { w: "data", def: "facts or information that scientists gather and study" },
      { w: "index fossil", def: "a fossil of an organism that lived for a short, known time and is found in many places — used to date rock layers" },
      { w: "rock layer", def: "a band of rock; lower layers are usually older than upper layers" },
      { w: "Paleozoic", def: "an era from 542 to 251 million years ago when early sea animals and amphibians lived" },
      { w: "Mesozoic", def: "an era from 251 to 65.5 million years ago when dinosaurs lived" },
      { w: "Cenozoic", def: "the era from 65.5 million years ago to today, when modern mammals appeared" }
    ],
    lesson: [
      "<h4>The Big Question</h4><p>Scientists use <strong>fossils</strong> to figure out what Earth looked like long ago — what kinds of plants and animals lived, what the climate was, and how things have changed over time.</p>",
      "<h4>Clues from Fossils</h4>" +
      "<p>Paleontologists gather <strong>data</strong> (facts) from fossils. For example, finding a fossil of a sea animal far from any ocean tells scientists that the area was once covered by water.</p>" +
      "<p>The <strong>age of a fossil</strong> is an important clue. Rocks form in <strong>layers</strong> over a very long time. In general, fossils in <strong>lower layers</strong> are <strong>older</strong> than fossils above them. If a fossil is found in a rock layer that is 65 million years old, the organism lived 65 million years ago.</p>",
      "<h4>The Fossil Record</h4>" +
      "<p>Scientists put together information from many fossils to form the <strong>fossil record</strong> — the total of all fossils, organized in units of time. The fossil record shows how organisms have <strong>changed over time</strong>. Scientists organized Earth's history into eras.</p>",
      "<h4>When Did Animals Appear on Earth? (Three Eras)</h4>" +
      "<p>Earth formed about <strong>4.5 billion years ago</strong>. Scientists divide geologic time into <strong>eras</strong>:</p>" +
      "<p><strong>1. Paleozoic Era — 542 to 251 million years ago.</strong> Early animals lived in the ocean. Jawless fish came first; later, jawed fish, amphibians, and reptiles appeared. About 252 million years ago, a <strong>mass extinction</strong> killed most land animals and plants.</p>" +
      "<p><strong>2. Mesozoic Era — 251 to 65.5 million years ago.</strong> <strong>Dinosaurs</strong> roamed the land, seas, and sky. Bird-like dinosaurs (like Archaeopteryx) lived in this era. About 65 million years ago, another extinction wiped out more than 70% of land animals — including the dinosaurs.</p>" +
      "<p><strong>3. Cenozoic Era — 65.5 million years ago to today.</strong> Modern mammals appeared. Monkeys and apes appeared between early mammals and today's mammals.</p>",
      "<h4>Index Fossils</h4>" +
      "<p>An <strong>index fossil</strong> is a fossil of an organism that:</p>" +
      "<p>• lived for a <strong>short, known time</strong><br>" +
      "• was <strong>plentiful</strong> at that time<br>" +
      "• is <strong>easy to identify</strong></p>" +
      "<p>If scientists find an index fossil in a rock layer, they immediately know about how old the layer is. The <strong>trilobite</strong> is a famous index fossil.</p>"
    ],
    quiz_mc: [
      { q: "What is the <strong>fossil record</strong>?", options: ["a list of dinosaur names", "the total of all fossils that have been found, organized in units of time", "a song about fossils", "a kind of rock"], answer: 1, explain: "The fossil record is the total of all fossils, organized by time, used to learn how life has changed." },
      { q: "If two fossils are found in different rock layers, which is usually <strong>older</strong>?", options: ["the one in the upper layer", "the one in the lower layer", "they are always the same age", "you can't tell"], answer: 1, explain: "In general, lower rock layers are older than upper rock layers." },
      { q: "Eva finds two cliffs with the same fossils. The fossils help her…", options: ["paint the rocks", "date the different rock layers", "build a fossil from clay", "find dinosaurs"], answer: 1, explain: "Scientists use index fossils and rock layers together to figure out when a layer formed." },
      { q: "Which fossil is an example of an <strong>index fossil</strong>?", options: ["a dinosaur tooth that comes from many time periods", "a trilobite that lived a short time and is found in many places", "an amber fossil that's very rare", "a footprint in dried mud"], answer: 1, explain: "Index fossils lived for a short time, were plentiful, and are easy to identify — like trilobites." },
      { q: "<strong>Dinosaurs</strong> lived during which era?", options: ["Paleozoic", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic", "Today"], answer: 1, explain: "Dinosaurs lived during the Mesozoic Era (251 to 65.5 million years ago)." },
      { q: "Modern mammals (and monkeys and apes) appeared during which era?", options: ["Paleozoic", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic", "Before Earth formed"], answer: 2, explain: "The Cenozoic Era — 65.5 million years ago to today — is when modern mammals appeared." },
      { q: "Early jawless fish, jawed fish, and amphibians lived during which era?", options: ["Paleozoic", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic", "Modern"], answer: 0, explain: "The Paleozoic Era (542 to 251 million years ago) is when early sea life and amphibians appeared." },
      { q: "About how long ago did <strong>Earth form</strong>?", options: ["4,000 years ago", "65 million years ago", "542 million years ago", "4.5 billion years ago"], answer: 3, explain: "Earth formed about 4.5 billion years ago." },
      { q: "What does it tell us if a sea fossil is found <strong>far from any ocean today</strong>?", options: ["The fossil is fake.", "The area was once covered by water.", "Sea animals can walk.", "The fossil is from the future."], answer: 1, explain: "Sea fossils far from oceans are evidence that the area was once underwater." },
      { q: "Which era ended with an extinction that killed most <strong>dinosaurs</strong>?", options: ["Paleozoic", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic", "Ice Age"], answer: 1, explain: "The Mesozoic ended about 65 million years ago when more than 70% of land animals — including dinosaurs — went extinct." },
      { q: "Which era is called the <strong>'Age of Reptiles'</strong> because dinosaurs ruled the Earth?", options: ["Precambrian", "Paleozoic", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic"], answer: 2, explain: "The Mesozoic Era (252 to 66 million years ago) is the Age of Reptiles — dinosaurs ruled the land, sea, and sky." },
      { q: "During the <strong>Precambrian Era</strong>, what kind of life lived on Earth?", options: ["dinosaurs and reptiles", "humans and large mammals", "only tiny, soft-bodied bacteria in the ocean", "fish and amphibians"], answer: 2, explain: "The Precambrian (4.6 billion to 542 million years ago) had very hot Earth with zero oxygen at first — only tiny bacteria lived in the ocean." },
      { q: "What is the <strong>'Law of Superposition'</strong> (Rock Layer Rule)?", options: ["bigger fossils are always on top", "lower rock layers are older than upper rock layers, like a stack of pancakes", "fossils on top are heavier", "all fossils are the same age"], answer: 1, explain: "Like a stack of pancakes, the bottom layer was made first — so lower rocks and fossils are OLDER than upper ones." },
      { q: "What percentage of all species that have ever lived on Earth are now <strong>extinct</strong>?", options: ["about 10%", "about 50%", "over 99%", "about 25%"], answer: 2, explain: "Over 99% of all species that ever lived are now extinct (gone forever). Extinction is normal in Earth's long history." },
      { q: "The <strong>Permian-Triassic</strong> extinction is called <strong>'The Great Dying'</strong>. About how much of all life died?", options: ["10%", "25%", "50%", "90%"], answer: 3, explain: "The Permian extinction was the LARGEST in Earth's history — about 90% of all life died, mostly because of huge volcanic eruptions." },
      { q: "What probably caused the <strong>dinosaurs</strong> to go extinct 66 million years ago?", options: ["a disease that only killed dinosaurs", "a giant asteroid hit the Earth, causing rapid climate change", "humans hunted them", "they slowly evolved into birds"], answer: 1, explain: "A giant asteroid hit Earth 66 million years ago, causing rapid climate change. About 75% of all life — including all big dinosaurs — went extinct." },
      { q: "<strong>Pangea</strong> was…", options: ["a giant ancient dinosaur", "one giant supercontinent where all continents were joined together long ago", "an extinct sea animal", "the name of Earth's first ocean"], answer: 1, explain: "Pangea was a supercontinent. We know it existed because identical fossils (like Mesosaurus) are found on continents now separated by oceans." },
      { q: "How do scientists KNOW that <strong>Pangea</strong> once existed?", options: ["ancient maps drawn by early humans show it", "the same fossils (like Mesosaurus) are found on continents now separated by oceans", "satellite photos show the old shape", "ancient books describe it"], answer: 1, explain: "Mesosaurus fossils appear in both South America AND Africa. Since the reptile couldn't swim across the ocean, the lands must have once been joined." },
      { q: "Why are <strong>trilobites</strong> the most famous index fossils?", options: ["they are still alive today", "they were abundant, found on every continent, and only lived during the Paleozoic Era", "they were the largest animals ever", "they are very colorful"], answer: 1, explain: "Trilobites meet all three index-fossil rules: lots of them, found everywhere, and lived for a short, known time (Paleozoic only)." },
      { q: "What is the <strong>#1 cause</strong> of most mass extinctions in Earth's history?", options: ["floods", "earthquakes", "climate change (too hot or too cold)", "human pollution"], answer: 2, explain: "Climate change — usually caused by volcanoes or asteroids — is the #1 reason most mass extinctions happened. Habitats are destroyed when the climate shifts too fast." },
      { q: "During the <strong>Paleozoic Era</strong>, what important thing happened on land?", options: ["dinosaurs first appeared", "humans built the first cities", "plants moved to land and bugs became HUGE", "Earth's first oceans formed"], answer: 2, explain: "The Paleozoic is the Age of Fish & Invertebrates. Life 'exploded' — plants finally grew on land, and bugs (like giant dragonflies) became huge." },
      { q: "Which era are <strong>WE</strong> living in right now?", options: ["Precambrian", "Paleozoic", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic"], answer: 3, explain: "We live in the Cenozoic Era — the Age of Mammals and Humans — which started 66 million years ago and continues today." }
    ],
    quiz_tf: [
      { q: "Lower rock layers are usually <strong>older</strong> than upper rock layers.", answer: true, explain: "True — rock layers stack up over time, so lower = older." },
      { q: "An <strong>index fossil</strong> must have lived for a long time across many eras.", answer: false, explain: "False — index fossils lived for a SHORT, KNOWN time. That's why they're useful for dating." },
      { q: "Dinosaurs lived during the <strong>Cenozoic Era</strong>.", answer: false, explain: "False — dinosaurs lived during the Mesozoic Era." },
      { q: "We are living in the <strong>Cenozoic Era</strong> right now.", answer: true, explain: "True — the Cenozoic Era began 65.5 million years ago and continues today." },
      { q: "The <strong>fossil record</strong> shows how organisms have changed over time.", answer: true, explain: "True — that's the main reason scientists organize fossils into a record." },
      { q: "Earth formed about 65 million years ago.", answer: false, explain: "False — Earth formed about 4.5 billion years ago. Dinosaurs went extinct 65 million years ago." },
      { q: "A trilobite is a famous <strong>index fossil</strong>.", answer: true, explain: "True — trilobites lived a short, known time, were plentiful, and are easy to identify." },
      { q: "Finding a sea fossil in a desert means the desert was always dry.", answer: false, explain: "False — it means the area was once covered by water." },
      { q: "<strong>Data</strong> means facts or information.", answer: true, explain: "True — scientists gather data from fossils to make conclusions." },
      { q: "The Paleozoic Era came <strong>after</strong> the Mesozoic Era.", answer: false, explain: "False — Paleozoic (542–251 mya) came BEFORE Mesozoic (251–65.5 mya)." },
      { q: "Over <strong>99%</strong> of all species that ever lived on Earth are now extinct.", answer: true, explain: "True — most species that ever existed are gone forever. Extinction is a normal part of Earth's history." },
      { q: "The <strong>Permian-Triassic</strong> extinction (the 'Great Dying') was the LARGEST mass extinction in Earth's history.", answer: true, explain: "True — about 90% of all life died, mostly because of massive volcanic activity." },
      { q: "<strong>Pangea</strong> was a supercontinent where ALL continents were joined together.", answer: true, explain: "True — we know because the same dinosaur and Mesosaurus fossils appear on lands now separated by oceans." },
      { q: "During the <strong>Precambrian Era</strong>, dinosaurs roamed the Earth.", answer: false, explain: "False — the Precambrian had only tiny bacteria. Dinosaurs lived during the Mesozoic Era." },
      { q: "The <strong>Law of Superposition</strong> says that lower rock layers are usually older than upper layers.", answer: true, explain: "True — like a stack of pancakes, the bottom layer was made FIRST, so it's the oldest." },
      { q: "A giant <strong>asteroid</strong> is the leading explanation for why the dinosaurs went extinct.", answer: true, explain: "True — about 66 million years ago, a giant asteroid hit Earth, causing rapid climate change and killing 75% of all life." },
      { q: "<strong>Climate change</strong> is the #1 reason most mass extinctions happen.", answer: true, explain: "True — when temperatures get too hot or too cold too fast, habitats are destroyed and many species can't survive." },
      { q: "<strong>Trilobites</strong> are still alive today.", answer: false, explain: "False — trilobites went extinct at the end of the Paleozoic Era. That's part of why they're such useful index fossils." },
      { q: "The Cenozoic Era is also called the <strong>'Age of Mammals'</strong>.", answer: true, explain: "True — after the dinosaurs went extinct, mammals grew big and spread everywhere. Humans eventually appeared too." }
    ],
    quiz_fill: [
      { q: "The total of all fossils, organized in units of time, is called the ___.", options: ["fossil mold", "fossil record", "rock layer", "tar pit"], answer: 1, explain: "Fossil record." },
      { q: "Dinosaurs lived during the ___ Era.", options: ["Paleozoic", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic", "Modern"], answer: 1, explain: "Mesozoic — 251 to 65.5 million years ago." },
      { q: "Modern mammals appeared during the ___ Era.", options: ["Paleozoic", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic", "Pre-Cambrian"], answer: 2, explain: "Cenozoic — 65.5 mya to today." },
      { q: "Lower rock layers are usually ___ than upper layers.", options: ["younger", "older", "the same age as", "softer than"], answer: 1, explain: "Lower = older." },
      { q: "A fossil that lived a short, known time and is plentiful is called an ___ fossil.", options: ["amber", "extinct", "index", "trace"], answer: 2, explain: "Index fossil — used to date rock layers." },
      { q: "Earth formed about ___ years ago.", options: ["4,000", "65 million", "542 million", "4.5 billion"], answer: 3, explain: "4.5 billion years ago." },
      { q: "Facts that scientists gather and study are called ___.", options: ["data", "guesses", "stories", "songs"], answer: 0, explain: "Data — facts or information." },
      { q: "The first early animals lived in the ___.", options: ["desert", "ocean", "sky", "mountains"], answer: 1, explain: "Early Paleozoic animals lived in the ocean." },
      { q: "About 65 million years ago, a mass extinction killed more than 70% of land animals — including the ___.", options: ["mammals", "trilobites", "dinosaurs", "monkeys"], answer: 2, explain: "Dinosaurs went extinct at the end of the Mesozoic Era." },
      { q: "The supercontinent that joined all continents long ago was called ___.", options: ["Pangea", "Asia", "Antarctica", "Eurasia"], answer: 0, explain: "Pangea — proven by matching fossils on lands now separated by oceans." },
      { q: "The very FIRST era of Earth's history is called the ___ Era.", options: ["Paleozoic", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic", "Precambrian"], answer: 3, explain: "Precambrian — 4.6 billion to 542 million years ago. Earth formed and only bacteria lived." },
      { q: "Over ___% of all species that have ever lived on Earth are now extinct.", options: ["10", "50", "75", "99"], answer: 3, explain: "Over 99% — extinction is normal in Earth's long history." },
      { q: "The 'Great Dying' (the largest mass extinction) was caused mainly by huge ___.", options: ["floods", "volcanoes", "asteroids", "humans"], answer: 1, explain: "The Permian-Triassic extinction killed 90% of life — caused by massive volcanic eruptions." },
      { q: "Scientists know dinosaurs went extinct mainly because a giant ___ hit Earth 66 million years ago.", options: ["wave", "volcano", "asteroid", "earthquake"], answer: 2, explain: "A giant asteroid impact caused rapid climate change. 75% of life — including all big dinosaurs — went extinct." },
      { q: "The Paleozoic Era is also called the Age of ___ and Invertebrates.", options: ["Mammals", "Dinosaurs", "Fish", "Humans"], answer: 2, explain: "Paleozoic = Age of Fish and Invertebrates. Plants moved to land and bugs got huge." },
      { q: "The ___ Era is called the 'Age of Mammals' — and it includes us today.", options: ["Precambrian", "Paleozoic", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic"], answer: 3, explain: "Cenozoic — from 66 million years ago to today." },
      { q: "The rule that says 'lower layers are older than upper layers' is called the Law of ___.", options: ["Gravity", "Superposition", "Fossils", "Layers"], answer: 1, explain: "Law of Superposition — like a stack of pancakes, the bottom is oldest." },
      { q: "The #1 reason most mass extinctions have happened in Earth's history is ___ change.", options: ["color", "climate", "shape", "rock"], answer: 1, explain: "Climate change — too hot or too cold — destroys habitats and causes mass extinctions." }
    ],
    challenge_questions: [
      { q: "Which of these conditions FAVORS fossil formation?", options: ["Rapid burial of remains under fine sediment in calm water.","Constant wildfire burning over an area for centuries at a time.","Decades of strong, dry wind blowing across a sandy desert.","Long-running pounding from heavy ocean waves on a rocky shore."], answer: 0, explain: "Wind, waves, and fire all DESTROY remains. Fine sediment quickly covering a body protects it from scavengers and oxygen — the right condition for fossilization." },
      { q: "Why does TIMING matter so much in fossilization?", options: ["Fossils form better when the weather is consistently warm year-round.","Timing of burial really doesn't matter much in the fossilization process.","Decay begins right after death and races against the speed of burial.","Older animals always tend to fossilize faster than younger ones do."], answer: 2, explain: "Bacteria and scavengers start breaking down a body within hours. If burial doesn't happen fast, there's nothing left to fossilize." },
      { q: "If older rock layers contain only sea creatures and newer layers contain land animals, what does it suggest?", options: ["The rock layers are upside down and need to be physically flipped over.","Land animals climbed up onto the older rock layers later in history.","Sea animals stopped existing on Earth at some point in deep history.","The area gradually changed from being underwater to being dry land."], answer: 3, explain: "The fossil record in successive layers shows how environments — and the life that filled them — changed over millions of years." },
      { q: "Why is Earth's history DIVIDED into ERAS like Mesozoic and Cenozoic?", options: ["Each major era of Earth's history is exactly the same length as the others.","Eras are mainly used as decorative labels in textbooks and museum displays.","Geologic eras are organized alphabetically by their official Greek names.","Major extinctions and big shifts mark natural boundaries between them."], answer: 3, explain: "Eras are bounded by big biological transitions — most famously, the K-Pg extinction (end of Mesozoic) when most dinosaurs died." },
      { q: "What's a MASS EXTINCTION?", options: ["Many species dying out in a relatively short geologic time period.","A perfectly normal day in the regular process of evolution on Earth.","A traditional festival held in many high-school biology classrooms.","A single species dying out by itself in a particular geographic region."], answer: 0, explain: "Mass extinctions wipe out a large fraction of life in a geologically short time — often triggered by asteroid impacts, volcanic activity, or rapid climate shifts." },
      { q: "Fossils show that some species existed for millions of years and then DISAPPEARED. What does this support?", options: ["The idea that life on Earth has changed dramatically over geologic time.","The idea that all known fossils are fakes manufactured by paleontologists.","The idea that the planet Earth itself is actually a brand-new creation.","The idea that biological species do not really change over deep time."], answer: 0, explain: "Disappearance of species in the record + appearance of new ones = direct evidence that life forms change over geological time." },
      { q: "Why are PATTERNS in the fossil record more powerful than single fossils?", options: ["Geologic patterns are simply easier for people to see than single fossils.","Patterns of fossils are formally required by international scientific law.","Patterns of fossils tend to look prettier when laid out for display.","A pattern across many fossils tells a story a single fossil cannot."], answer: 3, explain: "One fossil can be a fluke. A pattern across thousands of fossils shows reliable trends — like the gradual increase in mammal size after the dinosaurs." },
      { q: "Fossil FISH are found in the middle of the SAHARA today. What does this tell us?", options: ["Fossils have the natural ability to travel slowly across long distances.","The fossil fish swam underground through hidden underground rivers.","Some early people deliberately planted those fish fossils in the desert.","The Sahara desert region was once underwater in Earth's deep past."], answer: 3, explain: "The Sahara was once a shallow sea. Marine fossils preserve evidence of ancient environments, even when the present landscape looks completely different." },
      { q: "Why do scientists keep STUDYING the dinosaur extinction?", options: ["Studying ancient extinctions is a fun and entertaining academic pastime.","Past extinction events help us recognize warning signs of risks today.","Studying dinosaurs is popular with schoolchildren in many countries.","Studying extinction is technically required for many standardized tests."], answer: 1, explain: "Mass extinctions repeat across history. Studying past ones — what triggered them, what survived — helps scientists understand current risks." },
      { q: "What's a key piece of EVIDENCE for the asteroid theory of dinosaur extinction?", options: ["Preserved dinosaur footprints in mud at well-known fossil-bearing sites.","A worldwide layer of iridium plus a huge impact crater off Mexico.","Many old documentary movies about asteroids and ancient extinction.","Many small asteroid-shaped pebbles found scattered across continents."], answer: 1, explain: "Iridium is rare on Earth but common in asteroids. A thin iridium layer worldwide at the K-Pg boundary, plus the Chicxulub crater, point to a single huge impact." },
      { q: "Why do PEOPLE often think 'fossils = dinosaur bones'?", options: ["Other types of fossils besides dinosaur bones essentially do not exist.","Dinosaurs lived recently in geologic time, making their fossils common.","Movies, books, and popular culture greatly overemphasize dinosaur fossils.","Most fossils that are actually found in the field really are dinosaurs."], answer: 2, explain: "Pop culture loves dinosaurs, but the fossil record is mostly invertebrates, plants, plankton, and microbes spanning hundreds of millions of years." },
      { q: "Before dinosaurs existed, what kind of life was Earth's MAIN form?", options: ["Marine animals, early plants, and fish — for hundreds of millions of years.","Mammals very much like the ones that exist on Earth today filled the land.","Both birds and human beings were already established as the dominant forms.","There was no life at all on Earth before dinosaurs first appeared."], answer: 0, explain: "The Paleozoic era (before dinosaurs) saw the rise of trilobites, fish, amphibians, and the first land plants — long before any dinosaur existed." },
      { q: "Why are TRILOBITES so useful to paleontologists?", options: ["Trilobites are still alive today and easy to compare with their fossils.","Trilobite fossils tend to be unusually colorful and beautiful to look at.","They lived in many regions, evolved quickly, and help date rock layers.","The name 'trilobite' is easy for paleontology students to spell correctly."], answer: 2, explain: "Trilobite species changed often and were widespread. Finding a specific trilobite species helps date a rock layer — they're 'index fossils.'" },
      { q: "How can the fossil record HELP US PREDICT future biodiversity loss?", options: ["By providing entertaining stories with no real scientific use today.","By showing similar patterns from past mass extinction events on Earth.","By proving conclusively that extinction events never actually happen at all.","By giving scientists random data points to make educated guesses with."], answer: 1, explain: "Past mass extinctions show common signs: rapid climate change, habitat loss, food-web collapse. Recognizing them now is a kind of early warning." },
      { q: "Why is today's BIODIVERSITY connected to FOSSIL history?", options: ["The amount of biodiversity on Earth changes noticeably from week to week.","Fossils are mainly decorative objects with no real scientific connection.","Living species evolved from earlier species recorded in fossil layers.","Modern living species have no real connection to anything in the fossil record."], answer: 2, explain: "Every modern species has ancestors in the fossil record. The diversity we see now is the latest snapshot of an unbroken evolutionary chain." },
      { q: "What's the difference between MICROFOSSILS and MACROFOSSILS?", options: ["There is really no important difference between the two types of fossils.","Microfossils need a microscope to study; macrofossils are visible to the eye.","Microfossils are always significantly older than macrofossils in any layer.","Macrofossils are fake fossils that scientists use only for teaching purposes."], answer: 1, explain: "Microfossils (pollen, plankton, foraminifera) require microscopes. Macrofossils (bones, shells, leaves) can be seen directly. Both are crucial." },
      { q: "Why do POLLEN FOSSILS reveal past CLIMATES?", options: ["Pollen tends to be brightly colored, making it easy to identify in rock.","Different plants thrive in different climates; pollen marks what grew there.","Modern pollen is famous for making people sneeze during spring seasons.","Pollen actually originates from rocks rather than from any living plants."], answer: 1, explain: "Tropical plants produce different pollen than tundra plants. Finding pollen tells us what grew there, which tells us the climate of that time." },
      { q: "If a TROPICAL FERN fossil is found in Wyoming, what does that suggest?", options: ["Wyoming's climate has always been hot and humid throughout geologic time.","The fern was deliberately imported into Wyoming by early human settlers.","Wyoming was once a warm and humid climate at some point in deep history.","The fern fossil is a fake and was placed in Wyoming to mislead scientists."], answer: 2, explain: "North America's climate has changed dramatically. Tropical fossils in temperate regions show that Wyoming once had a much warmer climate." },
      { q: "Woolly mammoths went extinct only about 10,000 years ago. What likely caused it?", options: ["Their thick winter fur falling out due to a temporary climate change event.","A small meteor strike near a known mammoth grazing region in the Arctic.","A combination of warming climate after the Ice Age plus human hunting.","A sudden and unexplained lack of grass across the entire Northern Hemisphere."], answer: 2, explain: "Two pressures: post-Ice-Age warming reduced their habitat, and humans hunted them. Either alone might have been survivable; together they were too much." },
      { q: "What can fossils tell us about ANCIENT FOOD WEBS?", options: ["Tooth shape and stomach-content traces reveal who ate whom in the past.","Only what species existed in a region, but not how they interacted at all.","Nothing at all — fossils are silent on questions about ecological relationships.","Only the names of species, with no information about how they interacted."], answer: 0, explain: "Sharp teeth + stomach contents with bones = predator. Flat teeth + plant-fiber traces = herbivore. Fossils preserve relationships, not just bodies." },
      { q: "Why might a paleontologist DELIBERATELY look for fossils in a specific time period?", options: ["Gaps in the fossil record leave open questions that new fossils can fill.","Specific time periods don't really matter very much in paleontology.","Looking for fossils from specific time periods is required by their job.","Looking for fossils from a specific time period is mostly just for fun."], answer: 0, explain: "If we don't have many fossils from, say, 380 million years ago, we miss key transitions. Targeted searching fills evolutionary gaps." },
      { q: "Identical fossil species are found on Africa AND South America. What does this support?", options: ["Continents on Earth do not actually move or shift over geologic time.","Africa and South America were once joined as part of a single landmass.","Each species was able to swim across the Atlantic Ocean between continents.","Identical fossils on different continents are always misidentified or fake."], answer: 1, explain: "This is a classic clue for continental drift. Identical fossils on now-separated continents make sense if those lands were once part of one supercontinent (Pangaea)." },
      { q: "Why is the fossil record BIASED toward HARD-BODIED organisms?", options: ["Soft-bodied organisms decay long before they can be preserved as fossils.","Soft-bodied organisms simply did not exist on Earth long ago in the past.","Hard-bodied organisms are larger than soft-bodied ones in nearly every case.","Hard-bodied organisms are slightly radioactive, making them easier to find."], answer: 0, explain: "Skin, muscle, and internal organs rot quickly. Bones, shells, and teeth resist decay long enough to fossilize — so they dominate the record." },
      { q: "Which environment is BEST for fossil preservation?", options: ["An active volcano during an eruption, with flowing lava covering everything.","A fast-moving river carrying coarse gravel through a deep canyon.","A windy mountaintop with constantly shifting and exposed rock and dirt.","A still swamp where layers of fine sediment slowly pile up over time."], answer: 3, explain: "Swamps have low oxygen, fine sediment, and slow water — perfect for sealing remains before decay. Most major fossil beds form in similar low-energy, sediment-rich environments." },
      { q: "Why study ancient extinctions in light of TODAY's biodiversity crisis?", options: ["Past extinction events have no relevance to anything happening today.","Studying extinction events is fun and entertaining for paleontologists.","Past collapses show what causes major loss of biodiversity on Earth.","Studying ancient extinctions is required by most major colleges' programs."], answer: 2, explain: "Patterns from earlier extinctions — rapid climate shift, habitat collapse — match many warning signs scientists see today. History informs the present." },
      { q: "What's the boundary called between the dinosaur era and the mammal era?", options: ["The Stone Age period of early human history and tool development.","The Industrial Revolution period of major economic and social change.","The Big Bang event that took place at the very beginning of the universe.","The K-Pg boundary, short for Cretaceous-Paleogene boundary."], answer: 3, explain: "K-Pg marks the end of the Cretaceous (last dinosaurs) and start of the Paleogene (rise of mammals) — about 65 million years ago." },
      { q: "How might MAMMALS have BENEFITED from the dinosaur extinction?", options: ["Mammals didn't really benefit — most went extinct around the same time.","With large reptiles gone, mammals could expand into many new niches.","Mammals replaced dinosaurs as the dominant land animals in a single year.","Mammals were essentially unaffected by the disappearance of the dinosaurs."], answer: 1, explain: "Mammals had been small, nocturnal animals during the dinosaur era. After K-Pg, they radiated into many sizes and roles — including, eventually, us." },
      { q: "What can fossil TRACKS (footprints) tell us that BONES alone CAN'T?", options: ["The exact age in years that the animal had reached at the moment of death.","The actual day on the ancient calendar when the animal was first born.","The exact original color of the animal's skin, fur, scales, or feathers.","How the animal walked, how fast it moved, and whether it traveled in groups."], answer: 3, explain: "Bones show structure; tracks show BEHAVIOR. A trackway can reveal speed, gait, herding, even injuries — information bones alone don't preserve." },
      { q: "Why are some periods of the fossil record GAPPY?", options: ["Most of the fossils from those periods were stolen by treasure hunters.","Conditions favorable for fossil preservation were not always present then.","The Earth was completely empty of any kind of life during those periods.","Fossil records of those periods were intentionally destroyed by humans."], answer: 1, explain: "If sediments weren't being deposited, or if conditions were too harsh, fossils for that period are scarce. 'No fossils' doesn't mean 'no life' — just no preservation." },
      { q: "What's the BIGGEST conceptual shift that fossils give us?", options: ["A grasp of DEEP TIME — that Earth is billions of years old.","A general interest in rocks and rock formations on the Earth's surface.","An understanding of just one extinct group of animals: the dinosaurs.","A long list of strange Latin and Greek names of extinct organisms."], answer: 0, explain: "Fossils stretch our sense of time far beyond human history. Realizing life has been changing for billions of years reframes our place in the story." },
      { q: "If detectives find IDENTICAL Mesosaurus fossils in both South America AND Africa, what's the BEST explanation?", options: ["The Mesosaurus could swim across the entire Atlantic Ocean by itself.", "South America and Africa were once joined together as part of Pangea.", "Two completely separate Mesosaurus species evolved on each continent.", "Ancient humans deliberately moved the fossils between the two continents."], answer: 1, explain: "Mesosaurus was a small reptile that couldn't swim oceans. Identical fossils on now-separated lands prove the continents were once joined as Pangea." },
      { q: "Why is the <strong>Permian-Triassic</strong> extinction sometimes called the 'Great Dying' rather than just an extinction?", options: ["It killed about 90% of all life on Earth — a far worse loss than other extinctions.", "It is the most recent mass extinction event in the Earth's long history.", "It was the first mass extinction event ever recorded in the fossil record.", "It involved only large animals — small animals were not affected by it."], answer: 0, explain: "The Permian-Triassic killed about 90% of all life — by far the largest extinction in Earth's history. It nearly ended life on Earth completely." },
      { q: "Why did the <strong>asteroid impact</strong> kill the dinosaurs, even though many were nowhere near the crater?", options: ["The asteroid emitted radiation that traveled around the whole globe instantly.", "Dinosaurs all happened to die of natural causes in the same year by chance.", "It caused rapid global climate change — dust blocked the sun and cooled Earth.", "Each individual dinosaur received a personal mailed warning of the impact."], answer: 2, explain: "The impact threw huge amounts of dust into the air, blocking sunlight worldwide. Plants died, food webs collapsed, and 75% of life — including the big dinosaurs — went extinct." },
      { q: "How did the dinosaur extinction actually HELP mammals?", options: ["Mammals took over many empty roles in nature once the dinosaurs were gone.", "Mammals learned new behaviors from the dinosaurs before they all died off.", "Mammals were able to physically eat the dead dinosaur bodies for nutrition.", "The dinosaur extinction did not benefit mammals in any meaningful way at all."], answer: 0, explain: "With huge reptiles gone, mammals had room to grow bigger and fill new ecological niches — from tiny mouse-sized animals to mammoths, whales, and eventually humans." },
      { q: "Why is the current '<strong>6th extinction</strong>' different from the previous five?", options: ["Past extinction events were not actually real — only this current one is real.", "It is happening much more slowly than any of the previous five extinctions.", "It is happening only in the oceans, while the land remains entirely untouched.", "It is being caused by human activity — pollution and habitat loss."], answer: 3, explain: "The first five mass extinctions were natural events. The 6th — happening now — is driven by human activity, mainly habitat destruction, pollution, and climate change." },
      { q: "Why do scientists trust the <strong>Law of Superposition</strong>?", options: ["The law has been officially declared correct by major government agencies.", "Sediment piles up over time, so naturally lower layers were laid down first.", "It is just a guess — there is no actual evidence to support the rule.", "Scientists do not really trust the law of superposition very much at all."], answer: 1, explain: "Like a stack of pancakes, layers form from the bottom up. New sediment piles on top of older sediment, so the lower the layer, the older the rocks and fossils inside." },
      { q: "A detective finds a TRILOBITE fossil in a rock layer. What can they immediately conclude?", options: ["The rock layer must come from the very recent Cenozoic Era timeframe.", "The rock layer was once underground in deep-sea conditions only.", "The rock layer dates to the Paleozoic Era — that's when trilobites lived.", "The rock layer is one that was definitely formed in a desert environment."], answer: 2, explain: "Trilobites only lived during the Paleozoic Era. As an index fossil, finding one immediately dates the rock layer to 542–252 million years ago." },
      { q: "If you traveled back to the <strong>Precambrian Era</strong>, what would you find?", options: ["Dinosaurs walking around just like in popular movies and television shows.", "Early human ancestors hunting and using simple stone-age tools effectively.", "A hot Earth with lava, no oxygen, and only tiny bacteria in the ocean.", "Lush green forests filled with various mammals and large flying insects."], answer: 2, explain: "The Precambrian (4.6 billion to 542 million years ago) was a very hot Earth with no oxygen. Only tiny soft-bodied bacteria lived in the oceans — no big animals." },
      { q: "Why are <strong>volcanoes</strong> and <strong>asteroids</strong> linked to mass extinctions?", options: ["They both rapidly change the climate, destroying habitats faster than life adjusts.", "They each have been proven to directly kill living animals on physical contact.", "They both create big holes in the ground that animals are likely to fall into.", "They both make sounds loud enough to scare entire species into extinction."], answer: 0, explain: "Volcanoes and asteroids both cause rapid climate change — dust, ash, and gases block sunlight or trap heat. Habitats are destroyed faster than species can adjust, so they go extinct." },
      { q: "Why is the Cenozoic Era often nicknamed the '<strong>Age of Mammals</strong>'?", options: ["Mammals were the very first form of life that appeared on Earth.", "Mammals are the only kind of animal that has lived in the Cenozoic Era.", "After the dinosaurs went extinct, mammals diversified and dominated land.", "Mammals were physically larger than every other animal in this era."], answer: 2, explain: "After the K-Pg extinction (end of Mesozoic), mammals filled the niches left by dinosaurs. They grew bigger, spread everywhere, and now dominate land — including humans." },
      { q: "How do <strong>matching fossils</strong> on different continents prove that continents MOVE?", options: ["The fossils were physically pushed across the oceans by very strong winds.", "Animals like Mesosaurus couldn't cross oceans, so the lands must have been joined.", "The matching fossils are not actually real — they are clever forgeries by humans.", "The fossils each grew brand-new identical twins on each separate continent."], answer: 1, explain: "Mesosaurus was a small freshwater reptile — it couldn't swim across an ocean. The only explanation for identical fossils on Africa AND South America is that the lands were once connected." }
    ]
  },

  s73: {
    title: "Lesson 7.3",
    subtitle: "Living Things and Climate Change",
    emoji: "🌡️",
    cssClass: "sci",
    bigQuestion: "How can fossil evidence show that climate has changed and that some living things adjust while others go extinct?",
    vocab: [
      { w: "climate", def: "the usual weather pattern of a place over a long time" },
      { w: "argue", def: "to make a claim that is supported by evidence" },
      { w: "climate change", def: "a long-term change in Earth's usual weather patterns" },
      { w: "adjust", def: "to change or adapt to fit new conditions" },
      { w: "environment", def: "the surroundings where a plant or animal lives" }
    ],
    lesson: [
      "<h4>The Big Question</h4><p>Earth's <strong>climate</strong> has a pattern of <strong>warming and cooling</strong> over very long periods of time. Scientists know this because they have fossil <strong>evidence</strong>. Different organisms need different climates to survive — so when the climate changes, some live on, but some go <strong>extinct</strong>.</p>",
      "<h4>Changes Over Time</h4>" +
      "<p>From fossil clues, scientists know that environments change a lot over time. <strong>More than 250 million years ago</strong>, the Mojave Desert in California was covered by a <strong>shallow sea</strong>! The organisms that lived there were very different from those that live there today.</p>" +
      "<p>Then, between <strong>225 and 65 million years ago</strong>, the seas dried up and the climate became <strong>dry and windy</strong>. Sea organisms could not live there. <strong>Desert plants</strong> like cacti and yucca grew, and they provided food for animals.</p>",
      "<h4>Evidence of Climate Change</h4>" +
      "<p>Fossils of <strong>plant seeds</strong> are good evidence of past climate. Different plants need different climates. If scientists find a seed of a particular plant in a rock layer, they can <strong>infer</strong> what the climate was like when that plant was alive.</p>" +
      "<p>For example, Ice Age <strong>mastodon</strong> fossils have been found in Florida — telling scientists that Florida was once much colder than it is today.</p>",
      "<h4>Climate Change and Extinction</h4>" +
      "<p>Scientists <strong>argue</strong> — that is, they make claims supported by evidence — that climate change can cause <strong>extinction</strong>. After big climate changes, some organisms no longer appear in the later fossil record. The kinds of organisms that <strong>could not live</strong> in the changed environment <strong>became extinct</strong>.</p>" +
      "<p>For example, scientists found a fossil of an extinct <strong>crocodile</strong> in a desert in Wyoming. In ancient times, a huge lake covered that area. When the lake dried up, that kind of crocodile could not survive — it became extinct.</p>",
      "<h4>Why Some Survive and Others Don't</h4>" +
      "<p>Some kinds of organisms <strong>cannot survive</strong> even a small climate change — they need the exact right conditions. Other organisms can <strong>adjust</strong> to changes — they may move to new areas, eat different food, or change their behavior. Those that adjust survive; those that can't, become extinct.</p>"
    ],
    quiz_mc: [
      { q: "What is <strong>climate</strong>?", options: ["the weather today", "the usual weather pattern of a place over a long time", "the temperature inside your house", "a kind of rock"], answer: 1, explain: "Climate is the usual long-term weather pattern of a place." },
      { q: "Why do scientists believe Earth's climate has changed over time?", options: ["It's just a guess.", "They have fossil evidence of organisms from very different climates.", "TV news told them.", "Climate doesn't actually change."], answer: 1, explain: "Fossil evidence shows organisms from past climates that are very different from today's." },
      { q: "More than 250 million years ago, the <strong>Mojave Desert</strong> was actually a…", options: ["frozen ice cap", "shallow sea", "tropical jungle", "tall mountain"], answer: 1, explain: "Fossils of sea organisms in the Mojave Desert show it was once a shallow sea." },
      { q: "Scientists found an extinct <strong>crocodile</strong> fossil in a Wyoming desert. What does this tell them?", options: ["Crocodiles always lived in deserts.", "A huge lake once covered that area, then dried up.", "Crocodiles can fly.", "The fossil is fake."], answer: 1, explain: "The crocodile lived when the area was a lake. When the lake dried up, that kind of crocodile became extinct." },
      { q: "What does <strong>argue</strong> mean in science?", options: ["to fight with someone", "to make a claim supported by evidence", "to guess", "to tell a story"], answer: 1, explain: "In science, to argue means to make a claim supported by evidence — like using fossils to argue that climate changed." },
      { q: "Why do some organisms survive climate change while others go extinct?", options: ["They are lucky.", "They can adjust to the new conditions, while others cannot.", "Their bones are stronger.", "They are bigger."], answer: 1, explain: "Organisms that adjust — by moving, eating new food, or changing behavior — survive. Those that can't, go extinct." },
      { q: "Why are <strong>plant seed fossils</strong> good evidence of past climate?", options: ["They are colorful.", "Different plants need different climates, so the seed tells us the climate.", "They are easy to find.", "Plants don't fossilize."], answer: 1, explain: "Different plants need different climates, so a seed tells scientists what the climate was like." },
      { q: "What did scientists learn from finding <strong>Ice Age mastodon fossils in Florida</strong>?", options: ["Florida has always been warm.", "Florida used to be much colder than today.", "Mastodons swam in the ocean.", "Florida was once a desert."], answer: 1, explain: "Mastodons lived in cold climates, so finding them in Florida shows it was once much colder." },
      { q: "Between 225 and 65 million years ago, the Mojave Desert area's climate became…", options: ["warm and wet", "dry and windy", "icy and cold", "rainy and tropical"], answer: 1, explain: "The seas dried up and the climate became dry and windy — that's when desert plants like cacti and yucca grew." },
      { q: "Climate change can cause some kinds of organisms to…", options: ["double in number", "become extinct", "grow wings", "turn into rocks"], answer: 1, explain: "Organisms that can't survive in the new conditions become extinct." }
    ],
    quiz_tf: [
      { q: "<strong>Climate</strong> means the weather on a single day.", answer: false, explain: "False — climate is the LONG-TERM weather pattern of a place. Daily weather can change a lot." },
      { q: "Earth's climate has a pattern of warming and cooling over time.", answer: true, explain: "True — fossil evidence supports this." },
      { q: "All organisms need exactly the same climate to live.", answer: false, explain: "False — different organisms need different climates." },
      { q: "Scientists found <strong>sea fossils</strong> in places that are now deserts.", answer: true, explain: "True — like the Mojave Desert, which was once a shallow sea." },
      { q: "Some organisms can <strong>adjust</strong> to climate change and survive.", answer: true, explain: "True — they may move, eat new food, or change behavior. Those that can't go extinct." },
      { q: "Big climate changes have <strong>never</strong> caused extinction.", answer: false, explain: "False — big climate changes have caused many kinds of organisms to go extinct." },
      { q: "Plant seed fossils help scientists infer the past <strong>climate</strong>.", answer: true, explain: "True — different plants need different climates, so the seed identifies the climate." },
      { q: "In science, to <strong>argue</strong> means to fight without evidence.", answer: false, explain: "False — to argue means to make a claim supported by EVIDENCE." },
      { q: "Mastodon fossils in Florida show that Florida was once <strong>colder</strong> than today.", answer: true, explain: "True — mastodons lived in cold Ice Age climates." },
      { q: "If a kind of organism stops appearing in the later fossil record, it likely became <strong>extinct</strong>.", answer: true, explain: "True — that's exactly the evidence scientists use to argue an organism went extinct." }
    ],
    quiz_fill: [
      { q: "The usual weather pattern of a place over a long time is called ___.", options: ["weather", "climate", "season", "storm"], answer: 1, explain: "Climate — long-term weather pattern." },
      { q: "When organisms cannot live in a changed environment, they may become ___.", options: ["bigger", "extinct", "younger", "frozen"], answer: 1, explain: "Extinct — no longer living anywhere on Earth." },
      { q: "In science, to make a claim supported by evidence is to ___.", options: ["argue", "guess", "imagine", "wait"], answer: 0, explain: "Argue (in science) = make a claim supported by evidence." },
      { q: "Fossils of plant ___ are good evidence of past climate.", options: ["leaves", "stems", "seeds", "roots"], answer: 2, explain: "Different plants need different climates, so seeds tell scientists the climate." },
      { q: "More than 250 million years ago, the Mojave Desert was a shallow ___.", options: ["forest", "sea", "ice cap", "volcano"], answer: 1, explain: "Shallow sea — fossils of sea life prove it." },
      { q: "Ice Age ___ fossils have been found in Florida.", options: ["dinosaur", "mastodon", "whale", "dragon"], answer: 1, explain: "Mastodons lived in cold climates — finding them in Florida shows it was once colder." },
      { q: "Organisms that can adjust to climate change can ___.", options: ["survive", "explode", "disappear", "turn into fossils instantly"], answer: 0, explain: "Those that adjust survive; those that can't go extinct." },
      { q: "Scientists found an extinct ___ fossil in a Wyoming desert that was once covered by a lake.", options: ["dinosaur", "crocodile", "shark", "mammoth"], answer: 1, explain: "An extinct crocodile — the lake dried up and that crocodile could not survive." },
      { q: "Different organisms need different ___ conditions to survive.", options: ["climate", "color", "language", "name"], answer: 0, explain: "Climate conditions — different organisms need different climates." }
    ],
    challenge_questions: [
      { q: "What's the difference between WEATHER and CLIMATE?", options: ["Weather and climate mean the same thing in normal scientific use.","Weather only happens at night; climate happens during the day.","Weather is day-to-day events; climate is the long-term pattern.","Climate is local to one place; weather is a global phenomenon."], answer: 2, explain: "A rainy Tuesday is weather. 'Seattle is rainy' is climate. The key is TIME SCALE: short-term events vs. long-term averages." },
      { q: "Why can climate change DRIVE a species to extinction?", options: ["Animals tend to get bored when their environment slowly changes.","Climate may shift faster than the species can adapt to keep up.","Climate change makes most animals shy, so they hide and die out.","Climate doesn't really affect any wild animals on planet Earth."], answer: 1, explain: "Adaptation through evolution takes generations. If habitat conditions change in a few decades, many species can't keep up." },
      { q: "How do FOSSILS show that climate has changed BEFORE?", options: ["Fossil evidence proves that climate has actually never changed at all.","Most fossils are simply too old to show anything useful or reliable.","Fossils glow brightly whenever a planet's climate is changing rapidly.","Tropical fossils found in cold places show those areas were once warm."], answer: 3, explain: "Coal beds in Antarctica, palm fossils in Wyoming — direct evidence that today's climates are not what yesterday's were." },
      { q: "Which is a clear ADAPTATION to a COLD climate?", options: ["Bare skin combined with very short fur to keep cool in low temperatures.","Thick fur, fat layers under the skin, and hibernation during winter months.","Very large ears that release excess body heat into the cold air.","Brightly colored skin that attracts and absorbs as much sunlight as possible."], answer: 1, explain: "Cold-adapted animals need INSULATION (fur, fat) and energy strategies (hibernation). Bare skin and big cooling ears are HOT-climate features." },
      { q: "How are HUMAN ACTIVITIES connected to today's climate change?", options: ["Climate is completely unrelated to anything human beings do at any scale.","There is essentially no real connection between humans and climate change.","Burning fossil fuels releases gases that trap heat in the atmosphere.","Humans only affect ocean water, and not the atmosphere or temperature."], answer: 2, explain: "Burning coal, oil, and gas releases CO₂. CO₂ traps heat. Scientists have measured this directly for over 60 years — the link is well-established." },
      { q: "Why are POLAR BEARS at risk in a warming Arctic?", options: ["Their hunting grounds, the sea ice, are melting away as the Arctic warms.","Polar bears want to move south for reasons that are not yet well understood.","Polar bears have already gone extinct everywhere across the Arctic regions.","Polar bears strongly prefer warmer weather to the cold of the Arctic regions."], answer: 0, explain: "Polar bears hunt seals from sea ice. Less ice = less hunting = less food = trouble. Their entire lifestyle depends on a freezing climate." },
      { q: "What's a KEYSTONE SPECIES?", options: ["A species shaped roughly like a real metal key, often found near rivers.","A species whose role is so important that the ecosystem collapses without it.","The smallest species you can find living in any given habitat at one time.","A species that is most often kept in zoos for educational and visitor purposes."], answer: 1, explain: "Wolves in Yellowstone, beavers in wetlands, sea otters in kelp forests — without them, the whole system unravels. Their role is outsized." },
      { q: "Why are PLANTS so important when climate changes?", options: ["Plants don't really matter much to climate or to most ecosystems on Earth.","Plants only provide food for insects, not for any larger animal species.","Plants are pretty and provide pleasant scenery for ecosystems and tourists.","Plants are the base of food webs; if they fail, animals follow soon after."], answer: 3, explain: "Almost all energy in ecosystems starts with plants. If climate stress kills plants, herbivores starve, then predators starve. Cascading collapse." },
      { q: "Which is a NATURAL cause of past climate changes?", options: ["Volcanic eruptions and gradual changes in Earth's orbital path around the sun.","Modern sports cars driven for entertainment in many wealthy countries today.","Television sets in homes and businesses across the developed industrial world.","Modern smartphones used heavily by people in industrial countries today."], answer: 0, explain: "Earth's climate has shifted many times before humans, driven by volcanic activity, orbital cycles, and changes in solar output. Natural causes are real." },
      { q: "What makes CURRENT climate change different from past natural changes?", options: ["The current change is happening in only one country, not across the whole world.","The current change is happening at a much faster rate than past changes.","The current change is happening more slowly than past natural climate shifts.","There isn't any current climate change happening — it's a media exaggeration."], answer: 1, explain: "Past climate shifts unfolded over thousands of years. The current shift is happening in decades, giving species and ecosystems much less time to adjust." },
      { q: "Which is a typical adaptation for a HOT DESERT animal?", options: ["Eating only ice and frozen foods to keep their body temperature low.","Wearing a thick fur coat year-round to keep heat trapped near the body.","Hunting at night, when temperatures fall and water loss is reduced.","Sleeping right in the direct sunlight during the hottest part of the day."], answer: 2, explain: "Many desert animals (foxes, snakes, rodents) are nocturnal — they hunt at night to avoid the day's heat and conserve water." },
      { q: "Why are EXTINCTION RATES higher now than the historical average?", options: ["Animals around the world are bored, leading them to die at higher rates.","There really is no genuine increase in current global extinction rates.","Animals across the world are simply getting old at much faster rates today.","Habitat loss, climate change, and pollution all hit ecosystems together at once."], answer: 3, explain: "Multiple human pressures combine: habitat destruction, climate shift, pollution, over-harvesting. Together they create the highest extinction rates in millions of years." },
      { q: "What ROLE do OCEANS play in climate?", options: ["Oceans have no real effect on climate or weather patterns anywhere on Earth.","Oceans absorb heat and CO₂ and shape weather patterns across the world.","Oceans matter only to fish species and to a few kinds of large marine mammals.","Oceans matter only to people who live near beaches and on coastal islands."], answer: 1, explain: "Oceans store enormous amounts of heat and CO₂. They drive currents that move warmth around the planet. Without oceans, climate would look completely different." },
      { q: "Why are some animals MIGRATING earlier than they used to?", options: ["Animal migration calendars have been changed by international wildlife agencies.","The animals received entirely new migration schedules from biologists recently.","Spring conditions are arriving earlier each year as the world warms steadily.","The animals are confused for reasons that scientists cannot really explain at all."], answer: 2, explain: "Many migration cues (temperature, food availability) are shifting earlier. Animals are responding — sometimes successfully, sometimes badly when timing breaks down." },
      { q: "What is an INVASIVE SPECIES?", options: ["Any species that is unusually large compared with its closest known relatives.","A species that prefers to hide quietly rather than to interact with others.","A species that is unusually polite and well-mannered toward other animals.","A non-native species that spreads quickly and harms local ecosystems."], answer: 3, explain: "Brought from elsewhere by humans (sometimes accidentally), invasives often have no natural predators in the new place — and outcompete native species." },
      { q: "How can climate change make INVASIVE species WORSE?", options: ["Climate change cannot make invasive species worse in any meaningful way.","Warmer temperatures open new geographic regions where invasives can spread.","Invasive species die in warmer weather, so climate change actually helps a lot.","Climate change has essentially no effect on the spread of invasive species."], answer: 1, explain: "As regions warm, species that couldn't survive there before suddenly can. Climate change essentially expands the map for many invasives." },
      { q: "What is BIODIVERSITY?", options: ["The variety of life forms found in a specific area or across the Earth.","A particular type of fertile soil found in many tropical agricultural regions.","A relatively new science class taught at the high-school and college level.","A particular type of clean energy used in some industrial countries today."], answer: 0, explain: "Biodiversity covers species variety, genetic variety within species, and ecosystem variety. More biodiversity usually means a more stable, resilient world." },
      { q: "Why is BIODIVERSITY valuable beyond beauty?", options: ["Biodiversity is mostly important for tourism and the travel industry economy.","Diverse ecosystems tend to be more resilient and productive than simple ones.","Biodiversity is really just a fancy scientific word for color and beauty.","More diversity actually makes ecosystems weaker and harder to maintain."], answer: 1, explain: "Diverse systems handle disturbances better — losing one species hurts less when many others can fill its role. Diverse ecosystems also support more services humans need." },
      { q: "What is HABITAT FRAGMENTATION?", options: ["Large habitats broken into smaller pieces by roads, farms, or cities.","Soil that breaks apart easily into smaller fragments under foot pressure.","A small fragment of a science lesson on the topic of habitats and ecology.","Wild animals fighting each other in fairly small territorial pockets."], answer: 0, explain: "When highways, fields, or developments cut through a forest, animals can't move freely. Small fragments support fewer species and are more vulnerable to other stresses." },
      { q: "How can FOREST FIRES sometimes be HELPFUL to ecosystems?", options: ["Fires can clear old growth and trigger new growth in fire-adapted ecosystems.","Fires help only by warming things up briefly during the colder seasons of the year.","Fires help only during winter, when ecosystems most need extra warmth and heat.","Fires never really help any kind of ecosystem at any time of year, anywhere."], answer: 0, explain: "Some forests evolved with periodic fires. Lodgepole pinecones even need fire to release seeds. The problem is when fires become too frequent or too intense." },
      { q: "Why will HUMANS need to ADAPT to climate change?", options: ["Human beings are completely separate from natural systems on planet Earth.","Climate change has no real effect on cities or on the people who live in them.","Humans really don't need to adapt at all to any kind of climate change ahead.","Climate affects our food supply, water supply, health, and where it's safe to live."], answer: 3, explain: "Crops fail in some regions, sea levels rise where people live, heat waves get more dangerous. Humans are part of ecosystems and feel the same pressures." },
      { q: "What is RENEWABLE ENERGY?", options: ["Energy that is generated by burning coal in industrial-scale power plants.","Energy that comes from oil deposits in underground reserves around the world.","Energy that has to be reused over and over again from one main source.","Energy from sources that naturally replenish, such as wind and sunlight."], answer: 3, explain: "Wind, solar, geothermal, and hydropower draw on flows that don't run out on human timescales. Fossil fuels, by contrast, formed over millions of years and are finite." },
      { q: "Why do scientists drill ICE CORES from glaciers?", options: ["To check whether the ice in a particular glacier is real or somehow fake.","To use the harvested ice for making large amounts of ice cream and frozen treats.","Trapped air bubbles record the atmosphere from when the ice originally formed.","To collect frozen water from glaciers for drinking-water reservoirs in dry areas."], answer: 2, explain: "As snow falls and compacts to ice, it traps tiny pockets of ancient air. By analyzing those bubbles, scientists can read past CO₂ levels and temperatures." },
      { q: "How does PLANTING TREES help with climate change?", options: ["Trees release more CO₂ into the air than they absorb during normal growth.","Trees look attractive and add pleasant scenery to neighborhoods and cities.","Trees pull CO₂ out of the air and store the carbon as they grow over time.","Trees actually have no real effect on climate at any scale, large or small."], answer: 2, explain: "Through photosynthesis, trees take in CO₂ and lock the carbon into wood, leaves, and soil. Forests are major natural 'carbon sinks.'" },
      { q: "Why is climate change both a SCIENCE problem and a SOCIETY problem?", options: ["Climate change is really only a science problem and not a society problem at all.","Solutions need scientific knowledge AND coordinated human choices and behaviors.","Climate change is really only a society problem with no scientific component to it.","Climate change is neither a real science problem nor a real society problem at all."], answer: 1, explain: "Science tells us what's happening and what could help. But cutting emissions requires economic, political, and personal choices — not just lab work." },
      { q: "What's a meaningful action a CHILD can take regarding climate change?", options: ["Reduce waste, learn about the issue, share what they learn, and speak up about it.","Wait until they are at least 30 years old and then start acting on the problem.","Pretend that climate change isn't really happening at all in the real world today.","Nothing useful — only adults are capable of acting on the climate problem at all."], answer: 0, explain: "Kids have changed family habits, organized school programs, and pressured leaders. Small actions multiplied by millions of people add up." },
      { q: "What's an ECOSYSTEM SERVICE?", options: ["A particular type of business that operates within environmental fields and markets.","A government program designed to provide environmental services to citizens.","A subscription that you have to pay for monthly through a service provider.","A free benefit that nature provides, such as clean water or crop pollination."], answer: 3, explain: "Bees pollinating crops, wetlands filtering water, forests producing oxygen — these are ecosystem services. They're often invisible until they break down." },
      { q: "Why does LOCAL ACTION matter for a GLOBAL problem?", options: ["Local environmental action is technically illegal in most countries around the world.","Local action really doesn't matter at all when faced with a truly global problem.","Many local actions add up to large change, and local successes inspire others elsewhere.","Only the United Nations can take meaningful action on truly global environmental issues."], answer: 2, explain: "Cities cutting emissions, neighborhoods planting trees, schools reducing waste — these accumulate, and successful local efforts often spread to other places." },
      { q: "What's the BIGGEST OPEN QUESTION about future climate?", options: ["How quickly and broadly humans will choose to reduce their greenhouse gas emissions.","Whether plants will continue to exist on the planet over the next several decades.","Whether the sun will continue to rise each morning over the next several years.","Whether climate change is real and actually happening on the planet right now."], answer: 0, explain: "The science of warming is settled. The huge variable is human response — how fast and how widely we change our energy systems and habits." },
      { q: "Why is HOPE important when facing climate change?", options: ["Hope motivates action, while despair tends to lead to giving up on the problem.","Hope is technically required by international environmental law and regulations today.","Hope on its own makes climate problems disappear without any further effort needed.","Realistic hope can directly change the climate without any other action being taken."], answer: 0, explain: "People who believe action helps are more likely to take action. Cynicism and despair are corrosive. Realistic hope — paired with effort — is part of the solution." }
    ]
  }
};